qtanim - the animation demo, live in a window
=============================================

This program renders the somersaulting marble teapot of ../animation
over and over, into a window, instead of into image files.  It shows how
fast SIPP renders when nothing is written to disk.

The scene is the very same code (../animation/scene.c); only the output
differs.  By default the animation runs in real time: one loop takes a
fixed number of seconds (one, unless --period says otherwise), and
after each frame the renderer looks at the clock and renders the next
frame for the moment it will be shown.  A fast machine gets a smooth
animation of many frames per loop, a slow one fewer frames of the same
motion.  With --period 0 the animation's own 25 frames are rendered one
after the other as fast as possible, which is the mode for comparing
with the files ../animation writes.

Rendering runs on a background thread, with SIPP's own worker threads
under it; the window thread never calls SIPP.  Frames go into a
ring of three QImages.  A finished frame is published as the latest one
and the window is signalled; the window fetches the latest frame when
it gets round to it, so a display that cannot keep up skips frames
rather than queueing them.  The title bar shows the rendering and
display rates once a second, and each completed loop is reported on
standard output.

Building
--------

Qt 6 is needed (on macOS: brew install qt), and the SIPP library must
be built first.  From the top of the source tree:

    make qtanim

or by hand:

    cd demo/qtanim
    qmake
    make

qmake is /opt/homebrew/bin/qmake with Homebrew; "make qtanim" takes
QMAKE=/path/to/qmake if it is not on the path.

Options
-------

    -s, --size n          Image size in pixels (256)
    -j, --threads n       Rendering threads, 0 = one per core (0)
    -o, --oversampling n  Oversampling factor (2)
    -a, --shade-once      Shade each polygon once per pixel
    -p, -g, -f, -l        PHONG (default), GOURAUD, FLAT or LINE rendering
    -t, --period s        Seconds per loop of the animation (1.0);
                          0 = step through the animation's own frames
    --max-fps n           Render at most n frames per second (0 = no limit)
    --loops n             Quit after n loops, 0 = run until closed (0)
    --dump dir            Write the first loop's frames into dir as PNG

Example, a quick benchmark that does not need to be watched:

    ./qtanim -s 512 --period 0 --loops 5
